    primary: 'Two real sources now support this, honestly matched to what they actually show: Malir R, Chrudimsky J, Steffl M, Stastny P. A systematic review of dynamic, kinematic, and muscle activity during gymnastic still rings elements. Sports. 2023;11(3):50. doi:10.3390/sports11030050 — confirms static holds (iron cross, support scale) demand sustained high isometric/eccentric contraction and that training progresses from easier to harder static elements, matching this program\'s progression structure. Lum D, Barbosa TM. Brief Review: Effects of Isometric Strength Training on Strength and Dynamic Performance. Int J Sports Med. 2019;40(6):363-375. doi:10.1055/a-0863-4539 — supports isometric/static-hold training generally (less fatigue than dynamic work, joint-angle-specific strength). Neither paper explicitly frames straight-arm vs. bent-arm as two distinct trainable qualities — that specific framing remains coaching tradition, not something either citation directly tests.',
